2.56.m:
Fierce by name are you;
of you, your houses are in the front, the eastern direction;
of you, Agni is your arrows.
Be gracious to our two-footed, to our four-footed;
whatever weapons of you there are, whatever arrows of you,
homage to them, svāhā to them!

Corpse-eaters by name are you;
of you, your houses are in the south, the southern direction;
of you, the waters are your arrows.
[…]

Sovereigns by name are you;
of you, your houses are in the west, the western direction;
of you, desire is your arrows.
[…]

Abiding by name are you;
of you, your houses are in the north, the northern direction;
of you, Vāta is your arrows.
[…]

Higher by name are you;
of you, your houses are above, the upward direction;
of you, rain is your arrows.
Be gracious to our two-footed, to our four-footed;
whatever weapons of you there are, whatever arrows of you,
homage to them, svāhā to them!

2.57.m:
If this god came from heaven,
if from the mid-air, if whichever is earthly,
if the sacrifice is the heavenly world of the lord of the sacrifice,
to all of them with homage let us pay worship.

2.59.m:
Anoint him, O Ādityas, with wealth;
make him grow, O Agni, yourself growing;
let the threefold, causing prosperity, attend him;
unite him, O Indra, with virile strength!

Let the all-bearing earth protect you with gold;
let Agni save [you] with iron;
in concord with the plants, let silver,
being well-disposed, bestow brilliance upon you!

Threefold by birth was this gold born:
one [part] became dearest to Agni;
one of injured Soma flew away;
one they call the seed of the waters, the creator;
let that gold be yours for life!

From heaven let gold protect you;
from the mid-space let silver protect you;
from earth let what is made of iron protect you;
he has stepped forth into this stronghold of the gods.

2.60.m:
O companions of the dwelling, I commit this
treasure which Jātavedas shall bring;
the sacrificer follows safely;
recognize him in the highest firmament!

2.61.m:
Guiltless let this hero be, O tawny one;
protect him from the fetters of deceit;
O king, fully knowing, release the fetters [of him]
for whom I attend with oblation, with ghee!

2.63.m:
Restorer is your mother's name,
Restorer is your father's name;
and you too are Restorer;
as such, restore this with [your] auspicious [power].

2.65.m:
May your great brilliance spread up to heaven;
be fragrant, possessing good brilliance for your friends.
May King Varuṇa speak favorably of you;
and to him may you be a dispenser of shares with oblation.
Subdue rivals for a hundred winters;
let all the clans hum after you!

Those who bring you toll and who [bring] you tribute —
may Soma bend the kin to you;
may Agni, your yoke-fellow, speak favorably of you;
for him […]

Abundant with offspring, possessing good heroes,
advance forth, subdue rivals!
May Indra accompanied by the Maruts speak favorably of you;
and to him may you be a dispenser of shares with oblation.
Subdue rivals for a hundred winters;
let all the clans hum after you!

2.67.m:
When, O evil spirit, you approach her,
bestow wealth-increase upon her;
do not harm even her offspring;
roam elsewhere, do not be here!

2.72.m:
The metres are its pegs all around;
the stomas its body, the yajus its loose earth.
They call the kṣatriya fashioned from her;
do not fall down; hold [him] upon the summit!

Thrust away, shatter the hostility;
trample beneath your feet those who act resistlessly.
May rivals seeking to harm not harm you;
let your realm, your splendour be supreme!

2.74.m:
King Varuṇa has called you from the waters;
may this Soma call you from the mountains.
May this Indra call you from these clans;
having become a falcon, fly hither to these clans!

2.75.m:
Prajāpati is free from turning back;
he is free from turning back through offspring.
May he, free from turning back, make me free from turning back!

Indra is free from turning back;
he is free from turning back through manly strength.
[May he, free from turning back, make me free from turning back!]

Soma is free from turning back;
he is free from turning back through plants.
May he, free from turning back, make me free from turning back!

The waters are free from turning back;
they are free from turning back through Parjanya.
May they, free from turning back, make me free from turning back!

2.76.m:
May the full-of-sap [beasts] yield milk for me;
may the elephants yield milk for me.
May the winged [birds] yield milk for me;
may the deer yield milk for me!

May the Apsarases yield milk for me;
may the Gandharvas yield milk for me.
May all beings yield milk for me;
may Vāta yield milk for me!

May heaven and earth yield milk for me;
may the intermediate space yield milk.
May Indra and Agni yield milk for me;
may Dhātṛ yield milk for me!

2.77.m:
I hold your mind;
I [hold your] thought, I [hold your] vow.
May you be under my purpose;
may we two be joined in my thought!

Be minded towards [me], be minded here;
let your mind delight here;
let your mind delight in me!

2.85.m:
Victorious indeed, unvanquished!
Cast the embryos upon that one;
he himself enters into the fetter!

Let the sacrificial gift protect me from the south;
let the sacrificial gift protect from the left!
Let it protect from behind against piercing shots;
let the sacrificial gift protect from the front!

2.86.m:
I step onto the eastern direction;
let Agni help me for vigor, for strength!
May I become dear to the directions;
let the directions be free of enemies for me, ghee-faced!

I step onto the southern direction;
let Indra help me for vigor, for strength! […]
May I become dear to the directions;
let the directions be free of enemies for me, ghee-faced!

I step onto the western direction;
let Varuṇa help me for vigor, for strength! […]
May I become dear to the directions;
let the directions be free of enemies for me, ghee-faced!

I step onto the northern direction;
let Soma help me for vigor, for strength! […]
May I become dear to the directions;
let the directions be free of enemies for me, ghee-faced!

I step onto the fixed direction;
let Viṣṇu help me for vigor, for strength! […]
May I become dear to the directions;
let the directions be free of enemies for me, ghee-faced!

I step onto the upward direction;
let Bṛhaspati help me for vigor, for strength!
May I become dear to the directions;
let the directions be free of enemies for me, ghee-faced!

2.88.m:
Smite away the demon, away the adversaries;
break apart the jaws of Vṛtra!
Apart his fury, O Indra, Vṛtra-slayer, […]!

3.1.m:
To you let your kinsmen come, calling [upon you];
let Agni, a swift messenger, be generous!
Let wives and sons be well-disposed;
as a formidable one, look upon abundant tribute!

Run forward from the furthest distance;
propitious have Heaven and Earth become for you!
So this king Varuṇa has spoken;
he has summoned you here; come to this [place] here!

3.4.m:
Flowing against your will,
he enclosed you indeed;
Indra [enclosed] you with his powers, O goddesses;
therefore your name is water (vār) indeed!

One god stood by you,
approaching as you flowed;
‘The great ones have breathed out (ud-āniṣuḥ),’ [he said];
therefore you are called water (udaka)!

3.6.m:
Impelled, Indra [goes] along the slope with his two bay steeds;
let your vajra go crushing forward against the enemies.
Slay those facing toward [us], those facing with [us], those facing away;
make all their true [strength] destroyed!

3.8.m:
May the evildoers encounter its cruelty;
through sleeplessness the well-doers [reach] meritorious life.
You exhilarate heaven with highest kin;
you were born from the heated mind.

We know all your surrounding births of old;
we know, O Sleep, who is your overlord here.
Protect us here who are glorious for glory;
go far away with the hateful ones.

3.13.m:
Hither has come the parṇa-amulet,
mighty, crushing rivals by its might;
let the vigor of the gods, the sap of plants,
quicken sovereignty in me without negligence.

Let the Vaiśya be dependent,
as well as the Śūdra and the Ārya;
subject all of them, O parṇa;
make the folk dependent on me.

3.16.m:
The serpent did not become; the serpent destroyed the embryo;
the sapless one struck the rock.
It has been made sapless by the sacred formulation of poison;
therefore living you shall not escape!

3.19.m:
Sharpened is this sacred power of mine;
sharpened is my manly strength;
sharpened is the victorious dominion of those
whose purohita I am.

I sharpen their sovereignty;
I sharpen their power, manly strength, and force;
I hew off the arms of the enemies;
I sharpen my own people.

Let them fall down, let them be lowermost,
who would fight against our generous Indra;
I strike down the enemies with sacred power; I raise up my own people.

I sharpen their weapons;
I cause their sovereignty, rich in heroes, to increase;
may their dominion be ageless and victorious;
may all the gods favor their fierce thought.

3.20.m:
May Savitṛ, Vāyu, Agni, Tvaṣṭṛ,
and the Hotṛ fix this dwelling, knowing well;
may the Maruts sprinkle with udder-like ghee;
may King Bhaga establish our agriculture.

Mistress of the building, sheltering, pleasant,
a goddess, you were fixed in the beginning by the gods;
clothing yourself in grass, may you be kindly-minded;
bestow on us wealth, O fortunate one, rich in good heroes.

3.21.m:
Make this kṣatriya of mine increase, O Indra;
make him the sole bull among the clans;
strike out all his enemies;
subject them to him in the battles for supremacy.

May he be the lord of treasures among treasures;
may he be the clan-lord of the clans, the king;
upon him, O Indra, bestow great splendors;
make his enemy devoid of splendor.

Apportion to him in the settlement, among horses, among kine;
expel him who is his enemy;
may he be the king at the summit of dominions;
O Indra, subject every enemy to him.

3.23.m:
Associate together; drink together;
may your food be sweet together;
may all your vow be turned in one direction;
let your thought be common.

Agree together; let Indra be your observer;
may this Agni quench your wrath;
what slaughter of heroes or frightful [deed] there was,
may all the gods wash that away.

Peace may Bṛhaspati be to you;
peace both Heaven and Earth;
peace the intermediate space, and he who is accompanied by the Maruts;
peace may Aditi be to you, whose sons are gods.

May Heaven and Earth be favorable;
may the waters, the plants be favorable;
may all the fires be favorable
for his preeminence.

I unite your heart;
united let your mind be;
united let your bodies be;
united let your breath be.

3.25.m:
The draft-ox has sustained the earth and that sky;
the draft-ox has sustained the wide atmosphere;
the draft-ox has sustained the six wide directions;
the draft-ox has entered into this whole world.

The draft-ox yields milk in the world of the well-done;
Pavamāna [purifies] him from the front;
Parjanya is his streams, the Maruts his udder,
sacrifice his milk, the sacred gift his milking.

The draft-ox is Indra; he looks abroad upon the cattle;
the mighty one measures out the threefold paths;
milking the world, past and future,
he moves bearing all the vows of the gods.

These twelve nights, they say,
are the vow-nights of Prajāpati;
therein also is the vow of the brahman;
therein also is the strength of the draft-ox.

3.27.m:
Let this hero strike down foes without resistance;
all you gods, remove the seat [of rivalry]!
Let them not harm his offspring nor his men;
let Indra make him uncheckable!

Slay the foes, subdue [them]!
Agni is your herdsman, your best overlord;
let King Varuṇa extend protection to you;
even so let Indra make you uncheckable!

3.29.m:
Let me speak, not you;
in the assembly, indeed, you speak [only for me];
you shall be mine alone,
do not even mention other women.

3.30.m:
Together the kings have gone, together the debts have gone,
together the leprosies, together the evils have gone;
together with us is good dreaming;
we drive bad dreaming off to the one who hates us.

O womb of the wives of the gods,
agent of Yama;
what is auspicious dream, that is mine;
what is evil, that we send forth to the one who hates us.

3.32.m:
Let the knowing gods receive
the breath circulating from the limbs;
go to heaven, stand firm with bodies;
go to heaven by auspicious paths.

3.33.m:
Be kindled, O Agni, and awaken him,
and arise for great good fortune;
let your attendants here not come to harm;
let your brāhmaṇas be glorious, O venerable one.

You, O Agni, these brāhmaṇas choose;
be auspicious, a furtherer to us, O Agni;
be a slayer of rivals, O Agni, a conqueror of enemies;
shine in your own abode, unremitting.

Hold wealth right here, O Agni;
let the insidious ones not harm you through forethought;
let dominion, well-guided, belong to you, O Agni;
let your attendant increase unvanquished.

Take hold, O Agni, with your own dominion;
strive for friendship, O Agni, with a friend;
occupy the middle place of your kinsmen;
invoked by many kings, shine here, O Agni.

Cross beyond ambushes, beyond destructions,
beyond hostilities, beyond hatreds;
for you, O Agni, cross beyond all hardships;
then grant us wealth accompanied by heroes.

3.34.m:
O Agni, speak to us here,
be turned toward us, well-minded;
extend [wealth] to us, O lord of clans;
you are our giver of wealth.

May I speak cattle-winning speech;
cover me over with brilliance;
let Vāyu surround [me] on all sides;
let Tvaṣṭṛ be held for increase.

3.35.m:
From what we fear, O Indra,
make safety for us;
O bounteous one, be able [to help us] with your aids;
strike away the haters, strike away the assailants.

We call upon Indra for success;
may we succeed with two-footed and four-footed [creatures].
Let not ungenerous armies approach us;
scatter apart, O Indra, malicious spirits to every side.

Safety from a friend, safety from an enemy,
safety from the known, safety ahead of us;
safety by night, safety by day for us;
may all quarters become my friend.

3.36.m:
Cattle-winning are your arms, unequal in resolve in battle,
hundred-aided in every deed, swift-acting;
Indra has arranged incomparable strength;
as such, O Indra, have mercy upon us at our invocations.

May we conquer, supported with you as ally;
promote our portion in every battle.
O Indra, make broad space and easy going for us;
crush forth [the armies] of our enemies, O bounteous one.

3.37.m:
Cause his heart to burn; go, O desire;
let limb-fever burn him, let him grieve of himself.
Let his intentions meet with yearnings;
just this direction [is for him], not other directions.

3.38.m:
For the goat was born from the heat of Agni;
he beheld his progenitor in the beginning.
By him the gods attained godhood in the beginning;
by him, becoming more sacrificial, they ascended the ascents.

From the back of the earth I have mounted to the atmosphere;
from the atmosphere I have mounted to heaven;
from the back of heaven, of the firmament, I have gone to the heavenly light.

You are the goat; O goat, you are heavenly;
through you the Aṅgirases recognized the realm;
may we recognize that realm after [them].

[…] and cook five rice-dishes;
having cooked the goat with five rice-dishes,
the gods attained the realms.

3.39.m:
You are the kind husband, O Agni, the second;
create for her offspring reaching old age;
free her from Grāhi, from what bond of Nirṛti [holds her];
O Agni, bestow offspring upon her who desires offspring.

To you, O Agni, the bull, this eager [woman],
bending her knee, ministers, desiring a son;
mount her, being well-disposed;
unite her with the offspring of Prajāpati.

To you, O Agni, this woman desiring a son
offers pure, cleansed ghee;
mount upon her, show your virility, placer of seed;
fierce one, unite her with offspring.

When Indra was born, his navel fell away;
that one lustful god caught;
with you, we Brahmins, Soma-drinkers,
cause her who does not give birth to bring forth easily.

3.40.m:
From the shaft the arrow-point is born;
the shaft is born from the reed;
he neither frets nor grieves
who wears you, O shaft.

Let the waters place an arrow-point for me;
let the Maruts place an arrow-point.
Let all the gods place an arrow-point for me;
let the Fathers place an arrow-point.

Heaven has stood still; the earth has stood still;
this whole world has stood still.
It has stood still from the lower god;
let this desire of yours stand still.

4.2.m:
Having become, he deposits milk in existing beings;
he became the overlord of existing beings.
That death of yours performs the royal consecration;
let that king approve this kingship.

As he mounted, all surrounded and adorned him;
clothed in splendor, shining with his own light, he goes about.
Great is that name of the vigorous asura;
possessing all forms, he has attained immortal realms.

4.3.m:
Let him shine upon the five divine directions;
let him be the eldest among offspring like Indra.
Place in him abundant, variegated light;
let him fill the lap of the two realms.

Let Mitra and Varuṇa favor you here;
let heaven and earth [favor you] with plants.
Let the sun favor you with the days;
the moon with the constellations has favored you here.

4.4.m:
Lay hold, O Jātavedas;
subject [them] to the desire of [our] heart!
Arise as our messenger;
bring the sorcerers here!

This one has come praising [you];
welcome him indeed!
O Bṛhaspati, take [him] into your control;
O Agni and Soma, pierce [him] through!

4.5.m:
Bulls are your diggers;
a bull are you, O plant!
A bull are you, full of virility;
for a bull we dig you.

You are the sap of the waters, of the plants,
and of the trees;
and you are the brother of Soma;
you are virility from the buck antelope.

Gather the energies of the bulls,
gather the vigors of the plants;
gather virility, O Indra;
bestow bodily strength upon him!

Today, O Agni; today, O Savitṛ;
today, O goddess Sarasvatī;
today, O Brahmaṇaspati,
stretch his penis like a bow!

Upright stand the mountains;
upward the winds stir;
upright is this mine,
like a peg upon the earth.

4.6.m:
The wind blows not over the earth;
the sun does not burn past;
put all the people to sleep,
and the dogs, moving about as Indra’s ally!

Everything that stirs I have seized;
eye and breath I have seized;
I have seized all the limbs
in the deep dark of the nights.

Let the mother sleep; let the father sleep;
let the dog sleep; let the master of the house sleep;
let all the kinsmen sleep;
put all the people to sleep!

4.7.m:
From your eyes, from your nostrils,
from your ears, and from your mouth,
from the brain, from the forehead,
we tear away the head-yakṣma.

From your neck, from the nape-tendons,
from the breast-bones, from the spine,
from your shoulders, from the chest,
we tear away the arm-yakṣma.

From your lungs, from the two pericardial vessels,
from the gall-bladder, from the flanks,
from the kidneys, from the spleen, from your liver,
we tear away the yakṣma.

From your entrails, from the rectum,
from the rectum-caecum, and from the belly,
from the two hands, from the fingers,
from the fingernails we tear away the yakṣma.

From your bones, from the flesh,
from the sinews, from the veins,
from the ribs, from the marrow,
from the navel we tear away the yakṣma.

From your thighs, from the knee-caps,
from the heels, from the forefeet,
from your buttocks, from the pelvic region,
we tear away the hindquarters-yakṣma.

4.9.m:
“Abiding-wealths” is your name;
fierce-looking, realm-bearing indeed are the dice.
To you, O drop, may we pay worship with an oblation;
may we be masters of riches.

4.10.m:
Be superior to the mother-in-law;
teach your sister-in-law.
We make you glorious to your husband;
be dear to the brothers-in-law.

4.11.m:
I go with truth as my companion;
I know well the divine Anumati.
Let Indra be the heart of the gods for you;
let your mind, though fierce, be united!

4.12.m:
Like fire, O Manyu, conquer with speed;
invoked, O overpowering one, be our army-leader!
Having conquered the enemies, divide their wealth;
measuring out your vigor, thrust away the foes!

Overpower the enemy among us, O Manyu;
breaking, slaying, crushing, come upon the enemies!
Surely they did not check your fierce [might];
masterful, solitary-born, lead them into your power!

4.13.m:
May the divine Pṛśniparṇī make blessing for us,
misfortune for Nirṛti;
for she is fierce, crushing the Kaṇvas:
such, powerful, have I brought you.

4.14.m:
At sixty nights the enclosure of the sixtyfold
arrow-head was made;
from here, today, from its resting place,
we dislodge it for you.

4.15.m:
Let marrow be united with marrow,
and your joint with joint;
let what has slipped apart of your sovereignty be united;
united be sinew, and united your knuckle!

Let marrow be joined with marrow;
let [the bone] grow together over it!
We join your sinew with sinew;
let skin grow together with skin!

Let hair be joined with hair;
let skin fit together with skin;
let your blood grow with blood;
let flesh grow with flesh!

4.16.m:
Rising, let the sun kill the wood-worms;
setting, let the sun kill them with his rays;
so much for us […].

The Apsaras dug the root;
the Gandharva spoke around it;
by that has the sun, slayer of Vṛtra,
crushed the wood-worms in his jaws.

Approaching, let her kill the wood-worms;
departing, let her kill the wood-worms;
striking down, let her kill the wood-worms;
let her crush the wood-worms!

The eldest from the middle,
the youngest, and the middle one;
all your kinsmen are slain;
slain is mother, slain is father.

Down have the cows sat in the pen;
down have the wild beasts entered;
down, O sun, with your rays
have you destroyed all the wood-worms.

4.21.m:
The hairy-eared one ate you;
that he threw down […].
The girls of Bhīma made garlands of it;
all the poison sits on the buffalo.

From the broad [earth], of the broad-dweller,
of the swift and of the sick [man];
for by the earth's name:
let it ward off the poison!
Let it spoil the poison!

4.22.m:
My tongue is dripping with honey;
my tongue speaks sweetness;
O tongue, become brilliant!
[…] may the man be harmed.

4.23.m:
Stand upright, heedfully protecting this [man], O Unvanquished;
let not the Paṇis, the sorcerers, harm you!
Like Indra the Dasyus, shake down those who fight you;
overpower all rivals—
may the Unvanquished protect you!

4.24.m:
I know indeed your name, O fever;
Agni is your name.
As such we lead you away from here.

Slay the lowest demon, O Agni, as high;
strike down the middle one, shatter the highest!
May Agni with his face of light bring us welfare;
may heaven and earth bring us welfare, faultless.

4.25.m:
You are one of the golden [ornaments];
you were born from Soma.
You are conspicuous on the chariot;
you are shining on the quiver.
